blocks:
  - type: metin
    baslik: The core principles of cognitive behavioural therapy
    metin: |-
      The American Psychological Association (APA) describes CBT as resting on a few core principles: psychological problems are based partly on unhelpful ways of thinking and partly on learned patterns of unhelpful behaviour, and people experiencing these problems can learn better ways of coping with them ([APA](https://www.apa.org/ptsd-guideline/patients-and-families/cognitive-behavioral)).

      The word "partly" matters here. CBT does not say your thoughts are the only source of the difficulty you are going through. It focuses on the sources that can be worked on: how you think and what you do today.
  - type: metin
    baslik: 'The idea behind CBT: the cognitive model'
    metin: |-
      CBT is based on the cognitive model developed by Aaron T. Beck. As Beck Institute puts it, this model holds that the way we perceive a situation is more closely connected to our reaction than the situation itself ([Beck Institute](https://beckinstitute.org/about/understanding-cbt/)).

      An everyday example: a message you sent has gone unanswered for hours. If you think "they are angry with me", you get anxious and may keep checking your phone. If you think "they are probably busy", it soon slips your mind. The situation is the same; what changes the reaction is the meaning you give it.
  - type: kart-yigini
    kartlar:
      - baslik: How do thoughts, feelings and behaviour feed each other?
        metin: The three affect each other in a cycle. The thought "I will embarrass myself in this presentation" raises anxiety. Anxiety can lead to avoiding the presentation. Avoiding it brings relief in the moment, but it also stops the thought from being tested; at the next presentation the same thought comes back stronger. CBT looks at which link in this cycle can be changed.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: How does CBT work?
    giris: 'Work in CBT moves in two directions: patterns of thinking and patterns of behaviour.'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Noticing and testing thoughts
        metin: The first step is catching the thoughts that cause distress. Then how realistic these thoughts are is looked at together. At this stage the work covers recognising distortions in thinking and re-evaluating them against reality, understanding other people's behaviour and intentions better, using problem-solving skills in difficult situations, and building confidence in your own abilities.
      - baslik: Working on patterns of behaviour
        metin: On the behaviour side, methods include facing feared situations step by step instead of avoiding them, using role play to prepare for a conversation that may be difficult, and learning to calm the mind and relax the body.
  - type: metin
    metin: Not every course of CBT uses all of these methods. The psychologist and the individual make sense of the problem together and decide together which methods to work with.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: What happens in a CBT session?
    giris: 'CBT sessions are structured. This means the conversation does not drift at random:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: Each session has an agenda
        aciklama: At the start, what to work on that day is agreed together.
      - baslik: The focus is on the present
        aciklama: Some information about your past is needed, but the weight is on your current life and on ways of coping going forward.
      - baslik: It is collaborative
        aciklama: The psychologist does not tell you what to think; you examine your thoughts together.
    kapanis: Sessions last 50 minutes and usually take place once a week or once every two weeks. The length of the process depends on the individual's needs and the issue being worked on; no number of sessions is given in advance.
  - type: kart-yigini
    kartlar:
      - baslik: What happens between sessions?
        metin: In CBT the work does not stay in the therapy room. Small experiments are done between sessions, such as noticing a thought and writing it down, or trying a situation you avoid with one small step. These are not tests. If they do not get done, what got in the way becomes something to talk about too. The aim is that over time you can use these skills on your own, in a sense learning to be your own therapist.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: What is CBT used for?
    giris: 'These are the areas we often work on with CBT at our centre:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: '[Anxiety disorders](/en/blog/what-is-anxiety-disorder.html) and [panic attacks](/en/blog/panic-attack-symptoms.html)'
      - baslik: '[Social anxiety](/en/blog/social-anxiety-vs-shyness.html) and avoidance'
      - baslik: Obsessive thoughts and repeated checking
      - baslik: '[Low mood](/en/blog/what-is-depression.html) and loss of motivation'
      - baslik: '[Exam anxiety](/en/blog/coping-with-exam-anxiety.html) and performance anxiety'
      - baslik: Sleep problems and procrastination
  - type: metin
    metin: Our centre is not tied to a single theory. While CBT focuses on present patterns of thinking and behaviour, [psychodynamic therapy](/en/services/psychodynamic-therapy.html) tries to understand the roots of these patterns and recurring relationship patterns. [Acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT)](/en/services/act-therapy.html), instead of trying to change difficult thoughts, aims to take steps towards what matters to you even while they are there. Which approach fits depends on the person and the issue; that decision is made together in the first meeting.
  - type: metin
    baslik: Starting CBT
    metin: |-
      In the first meeting we talk about what brought you here, your expectations and what kind of work might suit you. If you would like to know what to expect beforehand, see our article on [what to know before starting therapy](/en/blog/before-starting-therapy.html) and our [therapy process](/en/therapy-process.html) page.

      Sessions can take place in Turkish or English, at our centre in Famagusta or [online](/en/services/online-therapy.html). Details of how we work with CBT are on the [CBT therapy](/en/services/cbt-therapy.html) page. For any questions, you can [get in touch](/en/contact.html).
